/-! ## (1) THE UNRUH / KMS PERIOD `β = azimuthalTurn / a` — the SAME closure as the Hawking period. -/

/-- THE UNRUH / KMS PERIOD `β = azimuthalTurn / a`. The imaginary-proper-time period at which the
    accelerated probe's near-horizon Euclidean section closes — DEFINED as the banked black-hole
    closure period `hawkingPeriod` (N360), with the acceleration `a` in the surface-gravity role.
    Rindler horizon = black-hole horizon: the SAME derived return-surface closure. -/
def unruhPeriod (a : Cut) : Cut := hawkingPeriod a

/-- ★ RINDLER HORIZON = BLACK-HOLE HORIZON (the moat, openly definitional): the Unruh/KMS period IS
    the banked Hawking period. One return surface, two physical horizons. -/
theorem unruhPeriod_eq_hawkingPeriod (a : Cut) : unruhPeriod a = hawkingPeriod a := rfl

/-- `unruhPeriod a = azimuthalTurn / a` — the period is one full derived Born turn over the
    acceleration. -/
theorem unruhPeriod_eq (a : Cut) : unruhPeriod a = azimuthalTurn / a := rfl

/-! ## (2) THE RINDLER ORBIT'S EUCLIDEAN SECTION and its KMS-PERIODIC CLOSURE. -/

/-- THE RINDLER-ORBIT EUCLIDEAN 2-PLANE POINT `(ρ·cutCos(a·τ), ρ·cutSin(a·τ))` — the near-horizon
    Euclidean section of the accelerated probe's worldline, the angular coordinate now the boost
    parameter `a·τ` (proper time `τ` scaled by the acceleration `a`). Built from the banked
    `eucPlanePoint` (N360). -/
def rindlerEucPoint (ρ a τ : Cut) : Cut × Cut := eucPlanePoint ρ (a * τ)

/-- ★ KMS-PERIODIC CLOSURE: the Rindler-orbit Euclidean section returns to itself under the
    imaginary-proper-time shift `τ ↦ τ + unruhPeriod a` (`= τ + azimuthalTurn/a`). The boost
    parameter advances by exactly one full derived Born turn `a·(τ + β) = a·τ + azimuthalTurn`, and
    the banked return-surface closure `eucPlane_closes` (N360, from the derived-trig periodicity N325)
    lands it back. This is the KMS periodicity — DERIVED as the fold's return on itself, not posited. -/
theorem rindler_closes (ρ a τ : Cut) (ha : a ≠ 0) :
    rindlerEucPoint ρ a (τ + unruhPeriod a) = rindlerEucPoint ρ a τ := by
  unfold rindlerEucPoint unruhPeriod hawkingPeriod
  have heq : a * (τ + azimuthalTurn / a) = a * τ + azimuthalTurn := by field_simp
  rw [heq]
  exact eucPlane_closes ρ (a * τ)

/-- ★ W8 NON-VACUITY (the KMS period is the FULL turn, not half): a half-turn `a·τ ↦ a·τ + cutPi`
    (at `a=1, ρ=1`, `τ` from `0` to `cutPi`) does NOT close — the shifted point `(cutCos cutPi,
    cutSin cutPi) = (−1,0)` differs from `(cutCos 0, cutSin 0) = (1,0)`. The sign flip IS the conical
    singularity; ONLY the full `unruhPeriod` closes. A wrong (sub-full-turn) KMS period genuinely
    fails — the temperature is not degenerate. Inherits the banked `eucPlane_half_turn_conical` (N360). -/
theorem rindler_half_period_conical :
    rindlerEucPoint 1 1 cutPi ≠ rindlerEucPoint 1 1 0 := by
  unfold rindlerEucPoint
  simp only [one_mul, mul_zero]
  exact eucPlane_half_turn_conical

/-- ★ THE SMOOTHNESS / KMS PERIODICITY: `a · unruhPeriod a = azimuthalTurn` — the acceleration times
    the period is exactly one full derived Born turn (no conical deficit). Inherits the banked
    `hawkingPeriod_smoothness` (N360). -/
theorem unruh_closure (a : Cut) (ha : a ≠ 0) : a * unruhPeriod a = azimuthalTurn :=
  hawkingPeriod_smoothness a ha

/-! ## (3) THE UNRUH TEMPERATURE `T = a / 2π`. -/

/-- THE UNRUH TEMPERATURE `T = 1/β` — the reciprocal of the KMS period, DEFINED as the banked
    `hawkingTemp` (N360) with the acceleration `a` in the surface-gravity role. -/
def unruhTemp (a : Cut) : Cut := hawkingTemp a

/-- ★★ `T_Unruh = a/(2·cutPi)` = `a/2π`. The temperature is the acceleration over one full derived
    Born turn `azimuthalTurn = 2·cutPi` (N328). DERIVED from the closure period, never posited.
    Inherits the banked `hawkingTemp_eq` (N360). -/
theorem unruhTemp_eq (a : Cut) : unruhTemp a = a / (2 * cutPi) := hawkingTemp_eq a

/-- Born positivity of the temperature: for positive acceleration, `0 < T_Unruh`. -/
theorem unruhTemp_pos (a : Cut) (h : 0 < a) : 0 < unruhTemp a := hawkingTemp_pos a h

/-! ## (4) ★ THE KMS-EQUILIBRIUM WELD: the equilibrium the probe reads IS the banked N375 maxent state. -/

/-- ★ THE KMS-EQUILIBRIUM WELD (the O9 content the two source modules never welded). For any
    observable `E` on a nonempty index set, the accelerated probe's equilibrium at the geometric-closure
